WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.650
The Joker is a character that's been reinvented
by some of Hollywood's most celebrated actors

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:09.870
for decades — from Jack Nicholson's cartoonish
take on the evil-doer in Tim Burton's classic

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:13.980
Batman, to Heath Ledger's iconic, dark portrayal
of the troubled villain in Christopher Nolan's

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.779
The Dark Knight, to Joaquin Phoenix's bleak
and realist interpretation in Todd Phillips'

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:19.779
Joker.

00:00:19.779 --> 00:00:23.220
Then there's Jared Leto's controversial Clown
Prince of Crime.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.289
"Introduce a little anarchy."

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:31.789
Leto took on the role in David Ayer's 2016
film Suicide Squad, and has now become the

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:36.150
first actor to get a second chance at playing
Batman's sworn enemy in a live-action movie

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.579
thanks to Zack Snyder's Justice League.

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:42.370
That's right: With the long-asked-for Snyder
Cut of Justice League, Leto has broken the

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:46.649
record for number of times an actor has played
the Joker in a live-action film.

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:50.060
Outside of live-action, of course, that's
a different story — but we're not talking

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.149
about that right now.

00:00:51.149 --> 00:00:57.149
"Even though you never call and never write,
I've still got a soft spot for you."

00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:02.210
Released in March on HBO Max, the Snyder Cut
orders up a second serving of Leto's Joker,

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:03.800
albeit with a few adjustments.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:09.250
The film gave Snyder the opportunity to take
back the reins of the 2017 film that was notoriously

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.680
reshot and re-edited by Joss Whedon.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.500
What he's delivered is a mammoth 242-minute
movie tailor-made to the director's wishes

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.750
— including the controversial inclusion
of Leto himself.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.240
But Leto's presence in the film was far from
a given.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.750
Here's how Leto's terrifying Joker came to
grace our screens with his presence once again.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:29.950
"All of that chit chat's gonna get ya hurt."

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:30.950
"Oh!

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:31.950
My god."

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.720
To put it lightly, Leto's Joker from 2016's
Suicide Squad never got the respect the method

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.979
actor had hoped for, with some fans calling
his performance as the iconic character the

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:42.770
worst one they'd ever seen.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:46.820
The film itself was widely panned, with the
actor's interpretation of the role seen as

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.799
a creepy, absurd, and thuggish caricature.

00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:54.060
While Heath Ledger was certainly a hard act
to follow, posthumously winning the Academy

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.560
Award for Best Supporting Actor in 2009 for
his take on the Joker — some fans believed

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.650
Leto deserved a second shot at the role.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.420
Suicide Squad director David Ayer did, as
well.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.850
In 2020, Ayer tweeted,

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.820
"My heart breaks for Jared – he did magnificent
work.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:08.970
Most of it remains unseen."

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.790
Later, referring to the editing process undertaken
by Warner Bros., Ayer doubled down on his

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:14.790
sentiments, saying,

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.890
"Jared was pretty mistreated during this.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.080
No one has seen his performance.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.980
It was ripped out of the movie."

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.290
It's understandable then, that Snyder and
his wife Deborah, who also served as a producer

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.560
on Justice League, had to do a little bit
of lobbying to get Leto's Joker included in

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.560
the new cut.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.190
As Deborah Snyder explained to The Hollywood
Reporter, they weren't sure how the idea would

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:34.860
go over with studio heads.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.780
She says they forged forward anyway, hoping
that the studio would be convinced when they

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.710
saw what the creative team had in mind.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:41.710
She said,

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:45.460
"At first, it was like 'No,' and then it was
like, 'Well, maybe,' and then Zack was like,

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.300
'Do you want to see the scene that I worked
on?'"

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:52.130
It's clear both Leto and Snyder took a cue
from the Suicide Squad criticism and decided

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.530
to tone down Leto's look for the Snyder Cut.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:58.910
Leto, like all of the other actors who've
played the Joker, is an Academy Award-winning

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:02.430
actor, clearly capable of taking the role
in a variety of directions.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.600
This time, he brought the character to a darker,
more subdued place.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.090
While the Joker of Suicide Squad was decked
out with short, neon green hair, flashy grills

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:14.910
on his teeth, and a body covered in loud tattoos,
Justice League's Joker displays a quieter

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:19.810
energy, emanating a sadness similar to Ledger
and Phoenix's performances that some are comparing

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.430
to Brandon Lee in The Crow.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:25.250
His hair is now long and black, and the tattoos
have (somehow) disappeared.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:29.300
In an interview with The Late Show's Stephen
Colbert, Leto called his new character "an

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.211
evolution" of what had come before, and credited
the new shades of his take on the Joker to

00:03:33.211 --> 00:03:34.660
Zack Snyder's direction.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.870
"When you work with a different director,
you bring out different things, so — or

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.980
they bring out different things in you."

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:44.090
While the meeting between Batman, played by
Ben Affleck, and Joker, was one of the most

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.450
highly anticipated moments of Snyder's film,
it turns out that the two actors didn't even

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.750
get to be in the same room during filming.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:52.430
Deborah Snyder explained to IGN,

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:53.850
"We had to shoot them separately.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:58.010
We had this ticking clock and we had to get
that scene shot because it had such extensive

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:01.150
visual effects but all of the actors' schedules
didn't align."

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:05.210
Ultimately, according to Deborah Snyder, they
got the go-ahead from the studio to include

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:06.210
the additional scene.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:10.030
"It was something that Zack was like, 'Listen
... this is the end of our journey here.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.340
I really want to see Batman and Joker.'"

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.900
"Careful."

